= Staal Considers the Assembly

Staal has governed by decree since the day he took the chancellery, and has never pretended to regret it. But within the <span style="color: #BFC8CC;">**GNU**</span> a different argument is gaining ground: that a tamed Assembly is worth more than a silent one. A majority of the right, formally seated and formally voting, would clothe the government in a legitimacy no Emergency Article can confer — at home, and in the eyes of the powers watching from the north.

Staal is unconvinced, but not unpersuadable. The question, he is said to have remarked, is not whether the chamber would obey; it is whether the chamber is worth the feeding.

Word reaches us that, should the numbers allow it after the next election, the general may accept a parliamentary coalition of the right rather than rule against the chamber. For the Assembly it would be survival of a kind — and domestication of another.
